 "answer": "In Corrective Retrieval Augmented Generation (CRAG), retrieved documents are assessed using a dedicated, lightweight retrieval evaluator—specifically a fine-tuned T5-large model (0.77B parameters)—rather than relying on a large, expensive LLM [C4, C5]. \n\nThe evaluator pairs the input query with each retrieved document individually to compute a continuous relevance score [C4, C5]. CRAG then applies an upper threshold and a lower threshold to these scores to determine an overall judgment across three discrete action triggers [C2, C6]:\n\n* **Correct:** Triggered when the confidence score of at least one retrieved document exceeds the upper threshold [C2]. Because reliable knowledge is present, CRAG accepts the retrieval and refines the documents into fine-grained \"knowledge strips\" by filtering out irrelevant parts and recomposing relevant information [C2, C6, C9].\n* **Incorrect:** Triggered when the confidence scores of *all* retrieved documents fall below the lower threshold [C2]. This indicates that none of the documents are relevant; CRAG discards the retrieved documents entirely and executes a web search to find external knowledge [C2, C4, C6].\n* **Ambiguous:** Triggered when the retrieval scores fall in the intermediate range between the thresholds [C2]. Because the evaluator is not confident in a binary judgment, CRAG takes a balanced approach by combining the refined internal knowledge strips with external web search results to complement each other [C2, C4, C6]. \n\nThis intermediate \"Ambiguous\" action was specifically introduced because relying solely on binary correct/incorrect decisions made the system overly sensitive to errors made by the retrieval evaluator [C2, C3].",
